---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DeletePdfPages from '@/components/tools/pdf/DeletePdfPages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="delete-pages-from-pdf">
  <DeletePdfPages client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to delete pages from a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above and wait for the thumbnails.</li>
        <li>
          Click each page you want to remove. Marked pages are dimmed, struck through and outlined in
          red. You can also type the pages in the range box, for example
          <code class="font-mono">2, 5-9, 20-</code>.
        </li>
        <li>The counter shows how many pages remain. It never lets you delete all pages.</li>
        <li>Press <strong>Delete</strong> and download the trimmed document.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Quick selections that save time</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[520px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Goal</th>
              <th scope="col">Fastest route</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Drop the blank backs of a duplex scan</td>
              <td>Click <strong>Even</strong>, then deselect the backs that have content</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Keep only the first chapter</td>
              <td>Type <code class="font-mono">15-</code> in the range box</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Remove a cover and a back page</td>
              <td>Type <code class="font-mono">1, last</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Keep a few scattered pages</td>
              <td>Select those pages, then click <strong>Invert</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Does deleting a page really remove the content?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. The remaining pages are copied into a new document; the deleted pages are not only hidden.
        Their text and images are not in the output, so nobody can recover them by scrolling, searching or
        examining the file. A redaction box drawn over content is different: it leaves the text below it in
        place, which is a well-known way to leak information.
      </p>
      <p>
        Resources shared with the remaining pages, such as an embedded font, are still copied. This is
        expected, because fonts are not private content.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Delete, extract or split?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Delete pages when most of the document stays and a few pages go.</li>
        <li>Extract pages when only a few pages stay, or when their order matters.</li>
        <li>Split when you want several documents from one, not one trimmed file.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All three run in your browser, and none of them changes the file you dropped in. Each gives you a
        new document to download.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
